SM(499,{"n":"Romans Introduction","a":"Fr Stephen De Young Archives/Romans Introduction.mp3","d":2956.2,"s":[[0.16,"Come and study Holy Scriptures with us as Father Stephen DeYoung teaches verse by verse on the podcast The Whole Counsel of God."],[8.08,"Father Stephen is a former Protestant pastor and Bible teacher in the Reformed Church, but now an Orthodox priest serving at Archangel Gabriel Orthodox Church in Lafayette, Louisiana."],[21.36,"Okay, so we'll go ahead and uh get started."],[25.36,"And uh we're actually at the beginning of a new book, so uh I don't have a lot of catch-up to the point where we're at to do."],[34.96,"Uh so this is uh tonight is primarily we'll see how long it takes us, but primarily gonna be an introduction, not just to the book of Romans that we're about to start, uh, but because this is the first of St."],[48.0,"Paul's epistles uh that we're gonna be reading."],[51.36,"It's also gonna be an introduction to St."],[52.88,"Paul's Epistles. So a lot of introducing, and then uh in future Bible studies, I'll be referring back to this one, uh, even though it'll take a while to be posted online."],[59.84,"So you'll just have to listen to that every week"],[67.0,"Um the epistles of uh Saint Paul, uh the word epistle basically just means letter, but it's a particular type of letter that had particular a particular form and a particular purpose and particular traditions surrounding it uh in the first century."],[90.04,"So uh epistles proper were written to groups of people."],[94.84,"Now there are epistles like 1st and 2 Timothy and Philemon uh of St."],[101.0,"Paul's that we have in the scriptures that were written to individuals."],[105.68,"And they're still they're still called epistles, they're still reckoned among St."],[109.84,"Paul's epistles. But properly speaking, it was it was to a group."],[114.16,"Now this would be true for any Roman person writing letters."],[120.06,"So, you know, a a Roman a Roman person writing a letter to Caesar wouldn't be writing a personal letter to the person Caesar, he'd be writing to Caesar's court to his government."],[133.42,"Um and uh vice versa, if he sent one to a governor, it would be to the you know, the governor and the other the other folk."],[142.78,"And so associated with the writing of epistles, we often have in our head, and this is gonna become important and we,'ll talk about. more this in a minute, when people talk about uh St"],[154.02,"Paul having written these epistles, and of course, some people are gonna argue that he wrote some of them and not others, uh, and what exactly that means."],[161.94,"We have in our head, you know, St."],[163.94,"Paul wrote these letters as if he sat down with a stylus and some parchment and wrote them out."],[172.44,"Which is not what happened."],[174.2,"Um all of them were written."],[176.92,"Uh we know this for a fact with all of St."],[178.92,"Paul's epistles, because at two different points in his epistles, he says, See with what large letters I'm writing to you with my own hand, which implies that other than those two times, he wasn't writing with with his own hand."],[192.04,"But what was common practice was there. were a group of what we would call scribes that were called in Greek an emanuensis"],[200.08,"And uh we have to remember that the literacy rate obviously is much, much lower at this point in history, and that reading and writing are different skills."],[211.68,"It's possible to be literate and be able to read a language without being able to write or write well in that language."],[220.88,"You've been to graduate school also, so you're aware of that."],[223.92,"You're aware of that."],[224.96,"Those are two different skills."],[226.56,"And so an emanuensis, his particular skill was being able to write well in a particular language."],[234.4,"And so the the person who was the author, of the letter would uh generally dictate uh the letter to the emanuensis, although dictate is a strong term in some cases."],[250.93,"In some cases, it would be virtually word for word."],[253.91,"Take this down, and then the amanuensis would still make corrections for style and grammar and and that kind of thing, polish it up."],[261.59,"Sometimes the author would just give sort of a general gist."],[266.45,"Mention the gift they sent me, uh remind them about this, tell them about this, and the emanuensis would be writing a great deal more of it."],[276.73,"We'd be responsible for more of the composition."],[279.45,"After uh he was done writing, then he would give it to the author to read, who would make any further corrections or changes they felt necessary, like no, that's not what I meant, or you know, clarify this, or I meant what I said, you shouldn't have corrected it, or whatever it was."],[297.69,"Uh and then that would be the final copy of that letter."],[304.09,"Uh in the case of uh Romans, if you go to the very end, uh, which is Romans chapter 16, uh, verse 22."],[321.21,"You'll notice it says, I, Tertius, who wrote this epistle, greet you in the Lord."],[326.57,"So this is a good trick Bible trivia question"],[331.17,"You ask someone who wrote the epistle to the Romans, and then when they say St."],[335.33,"Paul, you say no wrong Tertius, and you take them to this to this verse."],[340.13,"But uh so Tertius is the name of the Emmanuelsis who was involved in the book of Romans in particular."],[345.17,"Uh Tertius means third."],[347.09,"I know we mentioned this once before, uh, but uh Romans had four names."],[353.01,"Uh and your given name, your personal name was the least important of those names."],[358.53,"Um you would have your given name, your father's name, a family name, and then the name of an ancestor, usually your grandfather, but sometimes if there was some other illustrious ancestor, that would get put in there too."],[372.17,"And so since the given name was considered unimportant, often Roman men would give the names to their children Primus, Secundus, Tertius Quadratus, which means first, second, third, fourth."],[383.85,"It's just the birth order."],[386.33,"So their name would effectu uh effectively be, you know, the first son of the rest of the name."],[394.99,"Which would then be your father, most of your father's name."],[398.51,"Um so Tertius is likely the same, the same thing."],[403.47,"He was the third son of his father."],[405.79,"Uh, and this was his this was his job."],[409.07,"Uh there was then a a second person involved."],[413.19,"Because there was, of course, no Roman postal service."],[417.15,"You couldn't just write a letter and drop it in the mail"],[421.25,"Someone had to be assigned to take the epistle to its recipient."],[427.41,"And when that recipient was a group, that person who was sent would not only read the epistle aloud, but he would read it formally, and then he would be expected to be able to answer questions about it."],[443.09,"If people didn't understand something."],[447.05,"Have recorded who it was who brought uh the epistle to the Romans to Rome."],[453.13,"Uh, in some cases, we do."],[454.89,"For example, uh, at the end of 1 Peter, uh Sylvanus, Saint Sylvanus, from which the name Sylwon comes."],[463.21,"It's the same name uh in Greek."],[465.69,"Uh St. Sylvanus is is the one who brings First Peter to its to its audience and reads it."],[474.17,"Um so sometimes one or the other of those is identified, sometimes neither is identified in the actual text."],[482.17,"That person would have to be responsible."],[483.53,"They'd have to be at a uh they would usually be an associate of and at the end of St."],[490.81,"Paul's epistles, he'll also talk about some of the people who are quote unquote in the room."],[495.65,"It's other people who are there with him."],[497.61,"Um so in some cases, even though they're not identified, the person who brought it was probably one of those people."],[502.49,"He frequently mentioned Saint Luke, St."],[504.41,"Timothy, some of his other close associates."],[506.65,"And so it generally would have been one of them who would have brought it and then been able to, because they knew St"],[514.29,"Paul, answer, answer questions that they had."],[519.17,"So there are these two other these two other people involved in the process."],[523.97,"Not only this, but uh St."],[527.01,"Paul's epistles quickly begin to circulate."],[531.25,"He intended some of them to circulate."],[534.45,"So, for example, he tells uh the uh Corinthians that they should uh send their letter to the Laodiceans and get the letter to the Laodiceans and read it themselves."],[548.21,"So St. Paul was envisioning this."],[550.13,"It isn't just that they ended up getting passed around because people read them and saw how important they were, but that he envisioned, you know, even though he was writing to a particular church, that these would circulate at least in the nearby churches."],[563.77,"Uh so they begin to circulate, and as they begin to circulate, they also begin to be collected."],[570.34,"Churches, individual churches start to have more than one of them."],[574.18,"Uh probably the most famous case of at least two being put together is 2 Corinthians, which it's pretty clear that 2 Corinthians is actually made up of two of St."],[586.9,"Paul's letters to the Corinthians sort of stitched together in the middle."],[591.78,"You'll find some people, because dissertations have to be written, uh, arguing that there are as many as six different letters of the Corinthians that are stitched together into 2 Corinthians."],[602.54,"Yeah, and that's really stretching it."],[604.62,"But it's it's pretty clear there's a there's a pretty major shift where it seems to sort of conclude and then begin again in the approximate middle of 2."],[614.62,"And when we get to 2 Corinthians, we'll talk more about that."],[617.1,"So there's at least three letters to the Corinthians."],[619.66,"Well, we know there are that there are at least four."],[622.06,"Oh, okay. From what uh St."],[624.3,"Paul says in 1 and 2 Corinthians that he wrote them at least four letters."],[629.74,"Um so uh at least one we no longer have."],[634.22,"We also no longer have the Epistle to the Laodiceans."],[637.42,"Um so that's because we I know we talked about this once before."],[641.02,"'t That's because God doesn want us to have them."],[641.18,"And that's why"],[649.78,"So that shows the collection, though, that some church had the second and third letters, and so they put them together."],[655.62,"And they would have been put together for purposes of copying."],[659.48,"We've talked before about how expensive it was to hire, because of course, again, writing is a prize skill, to have a copy made of a letter."],[670.04,"To have a copy made of Romans, Romans is the epistle I use as an example because of its size, would have cost the equivalent in the first century of five thousand dollars in today's money to get a copy made."],[684.2,"So you could imagine if you have two different epistles from St."],[689.84,"Paul to the Corinthians that are of smaller size, you could see why you would want to just pay one fee and get one copy with both."],[701.52,"By the year 100 AD, all of St."],[706.56,"Paul's epistles had been collected together and were circulating as a unit."],[712.56,"In a book called the Apostolicon."],[716.88,"We have a copy, it's referred to as P4 6."],[720.64,"The P is for papyrus."],[722.68,"Um, and the 46th is because it's the 46th one they found."],[727.68,"It wouldn't have been called that in ancient times, but that's what it's called now, uh, by modern scholars."],[730.72,"That's from probably around 130 A.D"],[736.44,"That's a copy of that collection."],[739.16,"Uh does it have anything that's not now in the Bible?"],[742.44,"No. No, in fact, it has exactly what we have."],[745.24,"And it interestingly, it includes uh the Epistle to the Hebrews."],[750.6,"Um, and we'll talk more about that in a minute, but many, many people, not just today, you read the church, many of the church fathers doubted that St."],[758.28,"Paul had read, written the epistle to the Hebrews."],[761.08,"And the Epistle of Hebrews doesn't actually identify itself as being written by St."],[764.92,"Paul. Uh, so the primary reason why it was can broadly considered to be an epistle of St."],[770.76,"Paul is that it was circulating in this collection with all the other epistles of St."],[774.6,"Paul. And interestingly, also in P46 the Epistle to the Hebrews is not tacked onto the end."],[785.44,"It's actually the second one after Romans in our earliest copy."],[792.64,"That's because they were put in order by length from longest to shortest."],[799.04,"And that's where our order now comes from, too, although Hebrews has gotten moved toward the end because of those doubts about uh St."],[807.68,"Paul having written it."],[810.16,"Uh so they're they're very quickly collected and were operating in the church as a unit"],[816.8,"And the basis of our epistle book is the Apostolicon."],[820.64,"Because the readings at the earliest level, we know from St"],[826.36,"Irenaeus of Lyon that by 150 the four Gospels had been collected together as a unit and were being read in the churches as a unit."],[835.4,"And there would be a reading uh in worship from uh the Gospels and then from uh Saint Paul, from the Apostolicon."],[847.56,"Uh the other epistles, which are referred to as the general epistles or the Catholic epistles, uh, also were collected together into a unit by around 175, we know for sure, uh, because uh St."],[862.34,"Clement of Alexandria wrote a commentary on them. at that time"],[867.02,"And it was his commentary on them as a unit, so not as individual texts."],[873.1,"Um, and then they sort of all end up conglomerating into what we call the New Testament."],[879.66,"So uh that said, uh, as I mentioned, there are disagreements about which of these Saint Paul actually wrote."],[888.7,"Some of those are ancient disagreements, as I mentioned."],[891.82,"Um there were among the church fathers all kinds of suggestions as to who uh wrote the epistle to the Hebrews, uh, part mainly because the style of the writing is very different than the rest of St."],[907.26,"Paul's epistles, uh, even though a lot of the ideas seem very closely linked to a lot of St."],[913.06,"Paul's ideas. Uh, and so most of the most of their suggestions were people who were affiliated with Saint Paul."],[922.1,"So St. Barnabas was a common suggestion."],[925.62,"Saint Apollos, who was, as we saw in the book of Acts, was associated with Saint Paul."],[930.98,"Uh, the the oddest suggestion is uh that there was actually a a fairly obscure uh church father, a Fulgentius, who suggested that the Theotokos wrote it."],[943.7,"Uh I think we could safely set that aside, but it's interesting."],[947.7,"Um so there were all of these suggestions."],[952.18,"We still read it, when we read it uh in liturgy, we refer to it as St."],[956.46,"Paul's epistle to the Hebrews."],[959.26,"The epistle from St."],[960.22,"Paul to the Hebrews."],[961.9,"And here's why I'm going to argue that that's legitimate."],[966.3,"That we're not sort of fibbing or fudging."],[971.1,"Based on what I just described, in terms of how an epistle was written and how they were collected."],[979.34,"When we talk about Pauline authorship, when we talk about St."],[984.86,"Paul's authorship of these epistles."],[989.62,"Th'eres a sort of continuum."],[992.54,"There's a sort of range of what we mean."],[995.42,"We never mean he sat down and wrote it by hand."],[999.18,"But there are certain epistles, particularly the epistle to the Galatians, the epistle to the Romans, that we're beginning now, which everyone, from the most crazed fundamentalist to the most crazed liberal, agrees St."],[1015.78,"Paul wrote. That these are a direct reflection of St."],[1020.1,"Paul's mind. So while there was a scribe involved, this is pretty straight, mainline, direct, from Saint Paul."],[1030.24,"The other end would be, as we were just mentioning, Hebrews, which lots and lots of people all through the history of the church have kind of been dubious of at the other end of the continuum."],[1041.68,"In between, uh there are books uh more toward the Hebrews end would be what are called the pastoral epistles, which is uh 1 and 2 Timothy and Titus uh the ones to individuals, et cetera."],[1057.72,"Yeah, well, other than other than Philemon."],[1060.12,"Everybody's pretty pretty much agrees that Philemon St."],[1063.48,"Paul wrote. But yeah, they're dubious of 1 and 2 Timothy and Titus."],[1067.56,"Um, the epistles to the Ephesians and the Epistles to the Colossians, where you get people going both ways, and then there are people who are clear."],[1079.16,"But what you find when you read these texts closely, is when you get to, I'll start with uh Ephesians.ans and Colossi, which are kind of in the middle"],[1091.65,"When you read the introduction, all of the epistles have a formal introduction, and there was a way to introduce an epistle."],[1098.68,"So you'll notice if you go to the first page of Romans in Romans 1:1, it starts with you identify yourself as the author, Paul, a bonser to Jesus Christ."],[1108.74,"Because the rest of his credentials, and then he said, to who he's writing to."],[1113.6,"And then it goes on."],[1114.96,"If you look at um the epistle to Ephesians, the epistle to the Colossians, it doesn't just say Paul, it says Paul and Timothy to the church."],[1127.36,"So St. Paul listed a co-author."],[1130.1,"He listed at the beginning of the text that it was coming from him and from Saint Timothy."],[1135.34,"So the reason why in Ephesians and Colossians, the Greek style and these things don't match up quite as well to Galatians and Romans, is that say Paul had a co-author."],[1149.35,"So St. Timothy, and that's just taking the text as it's as it's introduced."],[1157.11,"What you find when you get to the pastoral epistles to 1 and 2 Timothy and Titus is that while the Greek is again different, and there's not a co-author listed, if you go through, and uh we could do this now very easily with a computer."],[1175.51,"If you go through and look at the phrasing that's different from St."],[1176.39,"look at But if you the things that are different that cause some people to doubt that St."],[1178.99,"Paul's usual phrasing, there's a lot of it that's the same."],[1185.23,"Paul wrote them, and you compare them to the rest of the Greek in the New Testament, what you find is they match up almost perfectly with St."],[1193.55,"Luke. And Saint Luke is mentioned in those epistles as being there with Saint Paul at the time they were written."],[1204.27,"And from what St."],[1205.23,"Paul says in those epistles, he was writing them from prison in Rome near the end of his life."],[1212.67,"So what we really see here is that it was likely Saint Luke possibly serving as the Emanuensis, or at least assisting in the production of these epistles."],[1221.71,"And so that's why some of the phrasing is a little different"],[1227.75,"But this is still coming from, still coming from Saint Paul."],[1234.31,"When we get to Hebrews, when we get to Hebrews, there's a question as to whether what we call the epistle to the Hebrews is actually an epistle."],[1246.63,"Because it does not start with that formula."],[1250.39,"As I mentioned originally, one of the reasons people doubted it, is that it doesn't say Paul to, you know, the church in Jerusalem or whoever the Hebrews in question in question would be."],[1263.81,"It immediately begins just with the text."],[1265.73,"the text. And if you if you look at the style of, which is different than St"],[1271.85,"Paul's usual style in his epistles, then what you see is that the style conforms very much to uh what was then current homiletical or rhetorical style."],[1285.29,"It looks more like a sermon than like a letter."],[1288.41,"And if you take that and you compare it to St."],[1290.81,"Paul's sermons in the book of Acts, you suddenly find a lot of crossover in phrasing and in syntax."],[1302.73,"Now, if that's because this was just St."],[1305.13,"Paul's preaching style, if that's because St."],[1306.89,"Luke was also maybe involved with transcribing in Hebrews, if it was a sermon preached by St."],[1312.69,"Paul. But that explains the difference in style and why there are all these ideas that resonate with St."],[1320.18,"Paul in the book of Hebrews."],[1322.58,"Now, Hebrews does conclude sort of like an epistle, even though it doesn't start like one."],[1328.1,"So the best explanation I'm going to argue, and I'll argue this more when we actually get into Hebrews, but the best explanation for Hebrews is that this is a sermon or homily of St."],[1340.42,"Paul's that has been transcribed and is now being sent and is circulating in printed form among the churches, and that that explains the differences."],[1355.9,"So I'm arguing that all of these come from Saint Paul."],[1362.56,"All of St."],[1363.36,"Paul's epistles are St."],[1364.56,"Paul's epistles, and then they come from St."],[1366.48,"Paul. But that there are different processes and different intermediaries, different scribes, different deliverers, different other people involved that account for the differences in style and format, these kind of things that we see among the different epistles."],[1390.16,"Paul's epistles in general."],[1392.36,"In terms of the Epistle to the Romans in particular, this is, as I mentioned, one that nobody doubts comes directly from St."],[1403.72,"Paul. St. Paul, at the time he writes the Epistle to the Romans, mentions that he has not been to Rome yet."],[1413.64,"And based on the context of the things he's going to talk about, he speaks a great deal about reconciliation between the Jewish Christian community and the Gentile Christian community."],[1429.08,"And so there's an event that corresponds to roughly when this would have been written that most, I think I can still safely say most."],[1440.36,"Most scholars think is uh forms the background of what's going on here."],[1445.24,"We actually read about it in the book of Acts"],[1447.84,"Remember in the in the book of Acts when we first met Priscilla and Aquila, the reason they were at uh Corinth when St."],[1455.92,"Paul met them there was that they had been expelled from Rome, along with the rest of the Jewish citizens by the Emperor Claudius."],[1463.02,"So the Emperor Claudius had expelled all the Jews from Rome."],[1465.5,"He actually did that twice."],[1467.42,"That's so effective the first one."],[1470.46,"Well, no, he'd later let them back in."],[1472.78,"He would relent and let them back in."],[1475.34,"And so uh around 54, we're not sure of the exact date, um, but it's around that period this expulsion happened, and shortly thereafter, within a year. uh Claudius allowed them to return"],[1493.06,"And so this return likely uh forms the background of this epistle, because the church in Rome had been started by uh Christian believers who had come following Pentecost."],[1511.63,"Who had returned to Rome, who were in Jerusalem on Pentecost because they were Jewish, and they were there to celebrate the Feast of Pentecost."],[1518.27,"They accepted Jesus as the Christ, returned to Rome, and that was the beginning of what amounted to at that point a Christian synagogue in Rome that later became the church, and then later Gentiles came in."],[1531.79,"Well, this initial group of Jews had all been sent out of the city by Claudius."],[1533.95,"of the And so the Gentile portion church had been functioning independently then for a period of roughly a year."],[1544.71,"And now these original Jewish Christians are coming back in and having to sort of reconnect with the community, and now the flavor of the community and things have changed."],[1556.71,"And what St."],[1558.39,"Paul does not want to happen is for this to end up breaking the community apart and having a Jewish Christian church here and a Gentile Christian church here that are two separate entities."],[1571.37,"He wants to see the unity of the church preserved."],[1576.09,"And so a lot of what we're going to read in the Apistle of the Romans is going to be aimed at that."],[1578.81,"It's going to be aimed at that"],[1582.53,"At pointing out that these two groups have found themselves in the same predicament and now find their salvation in Christ in the same way."],[1592.93,"Whether they were originally Jews or whether they were originally pagan Gentiles or they were Gentile God fearers, who, as we've talked about, are sort of somewhere in between."],[1603.65,"Sort of associated with the synagogue, but didn't go all the way to becoming Jewish."],[1609.89,"St. Paul has not been there yet at the time he writes this, which is different than many of his epistles that we're going to read."],[1617.41,"We read in the book of Acts, of course, about him founding the church in Corinth, for example."],[1622.29,"And so in many, and in Ephesus."],[1624.57,"So in many cases, he's writing an epistle to a community that he founded, sort of to give them further instruction and deal with problems there."],[1631.93,"Here he's writing blind to a community about situations he knows about, uh, but not to people who he, for the most part, knows directly."],[1645.69,"Why would he be well informed about the community in Rome, and why would the community in Rome expect a letter from him?"],[1657.53,"Or would they expect a letter from him?"],[1660.41,"Well, we don't know if they would have been expecting it or not."],[1662.73,"We know he had close associates like Saints Priscilla and Aquila, who we mentioned earlier, who were part of that Roman church and who had been expelled."],[1673.57,"So he had they know him, so they they knew he would have known about the situation through them."],[1687.57,"Additionally, remember Saint Paul sort of went back to Antioch and to Jerusalem, as we saw in Acts, as sort of a home base in between his missionary journeys."],[1699.33,"And he tended to go back."],[1700.85,"Remember, he went back for Pasca, he went back for another Pentecost."],[1705.81,"And so these were all times when Jews, including Christian Jews from all over the world, would go back to Jerusalem."],[1713.73,"They would have encountered him"],[1715.21,"So he would have heard from them then."],[1717.77,"They would have encountered the other apostles at that point, those who made that journey."],[1723.85,"So there was a fair amount of flux in and out."],[1729.45,"So that's how he would have known."],[1730.97,"But we will see."],[1732.25,"He knows about that general situation."],[1735.13,"Uh, but uh when he writes epistles to churches that he helped found and with which he's intimate, you'll see that he speaks about very specific situations."],[1745.61,"He'll name people, you know, who are causing a problem, or in a positive sense, you know, this person is a good leader, you should follow his way of life."],[1754.25,"Uh there's there's almost none of that in the epistle to the Romans because he doesn't have that level of uh familiarity uh with their situation."],[1764.85,"And that brings us to an important point uh about epistles, about reading St."],[1769.73,"Paul's epistles. In the New Testament, so far, you know, we've read gone through the Gospels and Acts."],[1776.59,"And those are all narrative."],[1779.57,"The Gospels, of course, are telling the story of Christ."],[1782.29,"Acts, as we saw, is uh St."],[1784.61,"Luke's continuation of the story of Christ and the Apostles."],[1788.77,"Uh epistles obviously aren't narrative."],[1791.11,"They're not telling a story."],[1792.84,"It's a letter."],[1793.8,"So that's different, but also, epistles are aimed at particular situations."],[1799.0,"They're what's what are called occasional documents."],[1801.96,"There was not a particular incident that happened that caused St"],[1807.12,"Luke to sit down and say, I really need to write a gospel and about the Acts of the Apostles."],[1814.8,"In general, people needed to know the gospel of Jesus Christ and they needed to know about the history of the early church, which he saw as a continuation of the gospel of Jesus Christ."],[1825.12,"Everyone in general."],[1829.2,"St. Paul's epistles are aimed at particular situations."],[1833.3,"A series of particular situations."],[1834.98,"As I mentioned, the Epistle of the Romans is a little less so than some of the others."],[1838.66,"I think it's directed toward that larger situation of the Jew and Gentile churches, but not, again, like like 1 1 Corint Corhians.inthians, where we'll see in and 2, where St"],[1851.18,"Paul is telling them to basically excommunicate a certain person because of sins he's been committing in the community."],[1857.98,"So it's not quite that level of particularity, but it's still in a particular situation."],[1862.14,"And the reason I make this point is many people, uh especially among our Protestant friends, uh, when they interpret the epistle to the Romans, uh, do not interpret it as an occasional document that's meant to address a particular situation."],[1877.66,"Uh some of them will even refer to it as St."],[1880.86,"Paul's systematic theology."],[1883.34,"They treat it as if this is a discourse on theology, that St."],[1888.72,"Paul was giving."],[1890.05,"Uh there's a way to write one of those."],[1893.45,"We have lots of treatises in the ancient world, including from the first century, from philosophers and that kind of thing."],[1899.13,"They're titled On Whatever."],[1901.05,"Yeah. On the Soul, on the Resurrection, on the Church Fathers write treatises like that."],[1910.54,"But St. Paul, if he wrote any treatises like that, they're not part of the New Testament."],[1916.38,"And we don't have them."],[1918.3,"They follow a very different format and a different structure."],[1921.39,"This is a letter to a church."],[1924.39,"So he's not talking about abstract theological ideas."],[1928.89,"He's talking about things that pertain to the situation that they're facing. right then at the time it was written"],[1934.57,"I keep thinking of our interregnum between when we were between priests"],[1942.21,"That the bishop might well have written us a letter telling us what to do and how to deal with certain situations and the situations that were arising, and it would be that sort of thing."],[1955.99,"And so it's it's aimed at that."],[1957.83,"Now, that doesn't mean that this is just a relic right from the first century that tells us about some problems that went on in the first century at the church in Rome, either."],[1969.09,"Because uh human nature is one, and the problems that they faced in the first century in churches are not so different from the problems that we've as Christians, the problems that people faced are not so different from the problems we face."],[1989.29,"And so we also can't read it as an artifact."],[1992.81,"It is also St."],[1993.53,"Paul addressing us."],[1995.71,"But he is addressing us in this particular pastoral way, not in a sort of rationalistic kind of let's muse about theology."],[2014.19,"Paul starts talking about things like election."],[2017.15,"And again, whether we understand those in an abstract way or whether we understand those as referring to particular particular situations."],[2026.15,"But we'll we'll get there."],[2027.67,"But that is something to always keep in mind."],[2030.07,"Something to always keep in mind."],[2031.75,"If our uh suggestion for what this means uh would have made no sense to a first century Christian in Rome, then we're wrong."],[2045.03,"Then we've gone off on some on some rabbit trail."],[2049.19,"Um as a last note, and then I think we'll get into the actual text a little."],[2058.39,"Um as I go through this, the way if you've if you're familiar with other treatments of the book of Romans, mine's gonna be very different."],[2070.11,"Because it's mostly been treated again by our Protestant friends."],[2075.95,"It was reading the Epistle to the Robins in Greek that really set off Martin Luther."],[2083.23,"It started the Protestant Reformation."],[2085.15,"So that's not a coincidence."],[2088.91,"And we'll, when we get to the passage in question, we'll talk a little more about this, but he made a particular application of part of Romans to the situation he was facing in medieval Europe with uh with Rome."],[2104.11,"Um, and we'll talk a little more when we get there about whether that's a legitimate application or not."],[2109.23,"But that application that he made of it shaped the way it's read uh by our Protestant friends, by our Roman Catholic friends in response to him for the last about 500 years."],[2123.99,"And even when we go to read the Church Fathers on Romans, for example, St."],[2131.11,"John Chrysostom, we have to remember that the translations we're usually reading of St."],[2135.35,"John Chrysostom were translated by mostly our Protestant friends."],[2141.35,"Like Philip Schaaf."],[2144.71,"And so they're translating St."],[2147.19,"John Chrysostom's commentary on Romans in line with their understanding of the book of Romans."],[2152.31,"Uh-huh Um not in a nefarious way, where they're trying to skew it, but it's the way that seems natural to them to understand it, because that's how they've imbibed it."],[2167.39,"So I'm going to be going at it differently than that."],[2173.39,"And one of the one of the biggest things that is shaped how I approach it, um, in addition to to the church fathers and and how they approach it, is the idea of uh watching how Saint Paul uses the Old Testament."],[2195.55,"Because as we're gonna see almost right off the bat, St."],[2197.95,"Paul is quoting the Old Testament constantly."],[2200.43,"Where he's not quoting it, he's alluding to it. it and referencing"],[2205.43,"And not just the Old Testament proper, but also the Jewish traditions of his time in which he's trained and through which he reads it."],[2215.67,"So we'll see little bits and pieces of that coming in too."],[2218.55,"That happens all over the place in St."],[2220.55,"Paul. Um of the famous examples are when he talks about uh in in 1 Corinthians that Israel had a rock that followed them through the wilderness."],[2231.19,"I didn't know that rock was Christ."],[2234.79,"We tend to focus on the rock was Christ, and we're like, oh yeah, the water comes through the rock."],[2239.11,"But we skip over that word followed."],[2241.59,"Like, wait, the rock followed them around?"],[2244.4,"Well, this is a rabbinical, this is a rabbinical tradition."],[2247.68,"It's from the what's called the Midrash"],[2251.08,"Because rabbis ask the question, well, wait a minute, at the beginning of their journey in the wilderness, Moses strikes the rock and they get water."],[2259.88,"And then they spend 40 years wandering around Sinai."],[2263.24,"And then at the end of that, Moses strikes the rock again and they get water, but he gets judged for it."],[2269.64,"Where did they get water for 40 years?"],[2272.44,"So the solution was, well, the rock must have followed them."],[2276.73,"And St. Paul just off-handedly mentions that the rock followed them in the wilderness."],[2283.95,"So the reason I bring this up is it's important in understanding how St."],[2288.19,"Paul sees the Old Testament."],[2289.95,"Because regardless of what uh our Protestant friends think about sola scriptura now, Saint Paul had no such idea."],[2298.53,"He understood the scripture, the text of scripture within this body of tradition that he'd received."],[2306.45,"And he didn't see a firm line between the two."],[2309.81,"We're like, well, yeah, there's some people who say for the rock followed him, but you know, the text just says that there was a rock at the beginning of the there's none of that with Saint Paul."],[2319.07,"He understands it within this tradition."],[2320.51,"There's bunches of other examples."],[2322.43,"In First Timothy, he talks about when Janus and Jambris contested with Moses."],[2327.71,"And you're sort of you can search all day through your Old Testament, you're not going to find Janus and Jambris."],[2334.99,"But according to the literature of the time, the religious literature of the time, those were the names of Pharaoh's court magicians."],[2343.83,"Who Moses had sort of the standoff with."],[2346.68,"Now, St. Paul isn't like endorsing all of those stories and saying all of these are scripture too."],[2351.78,"But he's also not saying, well, those names aren't, you know, in the Torah, so forget it."],[2358.87,"Well, if we were to refer to Fotini, Protestants would be completely puzzled."],[2365.73,"And that's where what I'm getting at."],[2367.57,"It's the same kind of thing."],[2369.03,"So if we're gonna, if we're gonna talk about what St."],[2373.03,"Paul's way of interpreting Scripture was, you know, and we want to compare it to the Protestant way or the Roman Catholic way or the Orthodox way, it's basically the Orthodox way of reading the Scriptures."],[2383.24,"Where there's not this separation and polarity."],[2386.9,"The Roman Catholic position is that Scripture and tradition are just two separate things."],[2392.12,"And that there's church authority that sort of picks and chooses what of each of them is valid."],[2399.24,"And then in the Protestant sense, there's just scripture and everything else is that tradition is one thing."],[2404.76,"picious, at least. Scri"],[2412.9,"Because the books themselves have been handed down to us."],[2416.16,"And it's tradition that says that these are the books that are scripture."],[2419.88,"Th areese the ones you read in church."],[2420.48,"So that's that's how how St"],[2423.88,"So there's not that separation."],[2427.08,"Paul approaches it."],[2428.44,"But I also have the bias that St."],[2430.52,"Paul interprets Scripture correctly."],[2433.08,"And that may sound bizarre."],[2435.25,"That I have to say that."],[2437.09,"But you would be surprised."],[2440.45,"Because there are whole books written on how the New Testament interprets the Old Testament."],[2446.53,"And they write about it as if it's an oddity."],[2449.94,"Because people today, modern scholars, modern readers say, W,ell we can tell what the Old Testament means."],[2456.1,"And then they read the New Testament and see how St."],[2458.58,"Paul and the other apostles quote the Old Testament and say, Well, they're doing something weird."],[2463.84,"Because our way of doing it clearly is the right way"],[2468.38,"And so they're doing something weird or wrong."],[2473.58,"More more conservative Christian folk who approach that will say, like, well, they were apostles."],[2480.46,"So God sort of gave them the authority to do that."],[2483.18,"But we shouldn't do that."],[2485.04,"Which amounts to God gave them the authority to do it wrong."],[2488.32,"You know. Which doesn't make sense to me either."],[2492.0,"So, but what that means practically is that as I'm going through and as we're reading and interpreting it, when we come to a quote from the Old Testament, my approach to that is not to say, okay, how do I think St."],[2503.92,"Paul is using it?"],[2505.68,"And then go back and say, oh, well, that must be. what the Old Testament meant"],[2511.24,"My approach is you go to the Old Testament, you see what the passage means in the Old Testament."],[2517.65,"And that shows you what Saint Paul is saying."],[2523.33,"To give an example from the Epistle of Romans that will come up later, and I'll talk more about it later."],[2529.81,"Our uh Calvinist friends like to quote St."],[2533.65,"Paul's quotation at the beginning of Romans 9, where he quotes from the prophet, Jacob, I have loved, Esau, I have hated."],[2540.69,"And then they structure a whole theology of predestination."],[2543.41,"Well, see, before they were even born, God loved one of them and hated the other one."],[2549.73,"Now, if you go back and actually look up that text from where St."],[2552.53,"Paul is quoting it, it has nothing to do with the people Jacob and Esau"],[2559.29,"It's talking about the nations of Israel and Edom who are descended from them."],[2565.29,"And how God had chosen Israel to be his people and not the Edomites."],[2571.05,"That's what it's about."],[2574.09,"That's what it's about."],[2574.97,"It's got nothing to do with the individual people."],[2578.97,"And like I said, we'll talk more about that when we get there, and even what the word hated there means with some other examples from scripture."],[2586.01,"But that's just an example of, you know, you you don don''tt take what you think St."],[2591.21,"Paul is saying and read back of the Old Testament."],[2594.19,"We take St."],[2594.75,"Paul's alluding to this, and we use that to understand the point that St."],[2597.31,"Paul is making"],[2603.91,"So that that approach is going to be one of the biggest differences, again, if you've heard other so the people receiving the epistle, at least the Jewish ones, would probably have pretty much the same interpretation of the Old Testament as Paul has."],[2623.03,"Yes, because they they were operating, and the Gentiles who were God fearers."],[2627.59,"Uh-huh. No synagogue"],[2629.91,"Because this is how they would have heard the Greek Old Testament."],[2634.63,"They weren't in Rome, especially, they were not using Hebrew."],[2640.07,"And uh they were not allowed uh to use Aramaic in the synagogues."],[2642.31,"You had two options"],[2647.31,"You could do Hebrew or you could do Greek."],[2650.91,"And so they were doing Greek."],[2653.47,"So they would have heard the Greek Old Testament tradition, and that's what they would have heard and heard preached."],[2660.29,"And so this is part of that aspect of St."],[2663.49,"Paul is not quoting the scriptures in some kind Hebrew scriptures in the vacuum."],[2670.26,"He's citing this tradition, this religious tradition of worship that's already going on."],[2676.08,"To which he has access, and to which his audience has access."],[2681.93,"So what he says refers to when Janus and Jambris contested it with Moses, they know exactly what he's talking about."],[2685.13,"We have some trouble"],[2688.23,"You know. We have to do a little more work and have a Bible study."],[2693.11,"But they had immediate immediate access to these things."],[2697.53,"They had heard these texts, these uh Old Testament texts preached."],[2702.83,"Because this is what was going on."],[2704.51,"Again, we still have Christian synagogues at this point, basically."],[2707.39,"And so this is what they're this is what they're hearing every every Saturday."],[2713.57,"Because remember, at this point in history, on Saturday, they're going to the synagogue."],[2717.61,"They're having readings from the Old Testament and preaching, and then on Sunday, just the Christians are gathering and celebrating the Eucharist."],[2728.05,"On the first day of the week"],[2730.14,"And then later in the second century, when the Christians are expelled from the synagogue, they move that part of the service on to Sunday also, and you get our divine liturgy with the two parts."],[2743.58,"So these are still separate."],[2745.82,"But they're that's what they're hearing."],[2747.18,"And the crit the preaching is Christian preaching."],[2750.16,"So they're they're hearing the Christian interpretation of that."],[2754.88,"No, you know, this sounds so more familiar for us before reading the X and the way they uh explained it, it it did not make more sense uh sense at the beginning."],[2766.08,"Like they have Saturday and they have Sunday, and where the Old Testament reading come from."],[2770.88,"Now it all makes sense."],[2772.0,"Um so one last note on Saint Paul, and then we'll get started"],[2772.84,"Good. Um this is more a reminder because we bed through the book of Acts."],[2783.8,"Um but we we read the story of his, and for people on the recording, I'm doing scare quotes, conversion."],[2791.8,"Uh at the way back at the beginning of of Acts."],[2796.2,"And remember, we talked about at that time that w we now look back on it, and Saint Augustine and Martin Luther looked back on it as a conversion, as if St."],[2805.4,"Paul leaves this one religion, this one religious tradition, and joins another religion."],[2810.52,"Okay. Well, St."],[2812.36,"Paul did that on the road to Damascus in thirty-five AD."],[2816.44,"So this is two years after Christ rose from the dead."],[2817.48,"So there was not Judaism and Christianity"],[2824.0,"It's two separate religions at the time this happened."],[2828.48,"Now there's obviously a change."],[2830.24,"If you mean conversion in the sense of repentance, then yes, that happened to St."],[2834.56,"Paul. He repented of what he was doing."],[2837.83,"But he did not see himself as having changed religions."],[2842.07,"He saw Christ as the Messiah who he had been expecting."],[2847.89,"He, as a Pharisee, had been expecting the Messiah to come."],[2851.25,"What changes is, oh, he has come."],[2854.02,"And he's Jesus of Nazareth."],[2856.74,"And then everything else is a dot for St."],[2858.9,"Paul as a domino that tumbles from that."],[2860.42,"from that. Well, if the Messiah has come and has died and has risen the dead, means we're in the last days"],[2866.46,"What's going to happen in the last days?"],[2868.62,"The Gentiles are going to turn."],[2870.24,"Is Israel's going to return to the land to God."],[2873.84,"The Gentiles are going to come streaming into Jerusalem."],[2876.8,"All of these things are going to happen."],[2879.36,"But those are things that he believed the day before the road to Damascus were going to happen."],[2885.28,"It's just now he's like, okay, well, now they're happening."],[2888.72,"So it's a shift in his timetable."],[2891.82,"And we're going to see once in Romans and once in another of his epistles, he refers to himself as a Pharisee in the present tense."],[2899.26,"So he doesn't see himself as having made this huge shift."],[2904.46,"And that's part of his approach."],[2907.32,"When he when he approaches uh his fellow Jews, it's not you need to abandon this false religion you have and come."],[2916.12,"It's the Messiah you've been waiting for has come."],[2921.4,"And this is who he is, and this is what that means."],[2926.2,"That's his approach."],[2927.24,"And so that they will make the same acknowledgement that he did."],[2933.8,"Listen next time as Father Stephen DeYoung continues his study of the scriptures on the Whole Counsel of God."],[2941.16,"Father Stephen's email address is wholecounsel at ancientfaith.com."],[2946.52,"That's whole counsel at ancientfaith.com."]],"o":[{"t":21.36,"lv":2,"h":"Intro to Romans and Paul's epistles","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Beginning a <b>new book</b> - no catch-up needed</li><li class=\"d0\">Tonight mostly <b>introduction</b>: to <b>Romans</b> and to <b>St. Paul's epistles</b> generally</li><li class=\"d1\">Romans = first of Paul's epistles we read</li><li class=\"d0\">Will refer back to this study in future Bible studies</li><li class=\"d1\">\"you'll just have to listen to that every week\" - slow to be posted online</li></ul>"},{"t":67.0,"lv":2,"h":"What an epistle is","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><i>Epistle</i> = <b>letter</b> - but a particular type: <b>form, purpose, traditions</b> in 1st c.</li><li class=\"d0\">Epistles proper written to <b>groups</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>1-2 Timothy, Philemon</b> written to individuals - still called epistles, reckoned among Paul's</li><li class=\"d1\">but properly speaking: to a group</li><li class=\"d0\">Roman custom: letter to <b>Caesar</b> goes to Caesar's <b>court, government</b>, not the man</li><li class=\"d1\">letter to a governor = to governor <b>and the others</b></li></ul>"},{"t":142.78,"lv":2,"h":"How the epistles were actually written","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">We picture Paul with <b>stylus and parchment</b> - <b>not what happened</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Proof: he twice says <b>\"see with what large letters I'm writing to you with my own hand\"</b></li><li class=\"d1\">→ implies at other times <b>not</b> his own hand</li><li class=\"d0\">Common practice: <b>scribes</b> = <i>amanuensis</i> (Greek)</li><li class=\"d1\">literacy rate much lower; <b>reading and writing are different skills</b></li><li class=\"d1\">amanuensis' skill = <b>writing well</b> in a language</li><li class=\"d0\">Author <b>dictates</b> - \"dictate\" a strong term at times</li><li class=\"d1\">sometimes <b>word for word</b>; scribe still corrects <b>style and grammar</b>, polishes</li><li class=\"d1\">sometimes author gives <b>general gist</b> (\"mention the gift they sent me\") → scribe composes much more</li><li class=\"d0\">Then author reads, makes <b>final corrections</b> - \"that's not what I meant\" - that's the <b>final copy</b></li></ul>"},{"t":304.09,"lv":3,"h":"Romans 16:22 - Tertius","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>Romans 16:22</b>: \"I, <b>Tertius</b>, who wrote this epistle, greet you in the Lord\"</li><li class=\"d1\">Bible trivia: \"who wrote Romans?\" - not Paul, <b>Tertius</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><i>Tertius</i> = <b>third</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Romans had <b>four names</b>: given name, father's name, family name, ancestor's name (usually grandfather)</li><li class=\"d1\">given name least important → sons named <b>Primus, Secundus, Tertius, Quadratus</b> = first, second, third, fourth</li><li class=\"d1\">just <b>birth order</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Tertius likely <b>third son</b> of his father</li><li class=\"d0\">This was his <b>job</b> - scribe for Romans</li></ul>"},{"t":409.07,"lv":3,"h":"The courier who read it aloud","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">A <b>second person</b>: no Roman postal service</li><li class=\"d0\">Someone assigned to <b>carry the epistle</b> to recipient</li><li class=\"d0\">For a group: <b>reads it aloud formally</b></li><li class=\"d1\">expected to <b>answer questions</b> if people didn't understand</li><li class=\"d0\">Not always recorded who brought it</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>1 Peter</b>: <b>St. Sylvanus</b> (Silas / Silvanus - same Greek name) brings and reads it</li><li class=\"d1\">Romans courier not named</li><li class=\"d0\">Likely one of Paul's associates \"in the room\" - <b>Luke, Timothy</b> - knew Paul, could answer questions</li></ul>"},{"t":523.97,"lv":2,"h":"Circulation and collection of the epistles","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Epistles <b>quickly circulate</b> - Paul <b>intended</b> some to</li><li class=\"d1\">tells Corinthians to <b>send their letter to the Laodiceans</b>, get theirs and read it</li><li class=\"d1\">envisioned circulating at least among <b>nearby churches</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Then <b>collected</b> - churches have more than one</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>2 Corinthians</b> = two Pauline letters <b>stitched together</b></li><li class=\"d1\">clear shift in the middle where it concludes and begins again</li><li class=\"d1\">some argue as many as <b>six</b> - \"dissertations have to be written\" - \"really stretching it\"</li><li class=\"d0\">We know of <b>at least four</b> letters to Corinthians from what Paul says → <b>at least one lost</b></li><li class=\"d1\">also lost: <b>Epistle to the Laodiceans</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Collecting shows a church had the 2nd and 3rd letters and joined them</li></ul>"},{"t":655.62,"lv":3,"h":"Cost of copying","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Joined for <b>copying</b> - writing is a prized skill, copies <b>expensive</b></li><li class=\"d0\">A copy of <b>Romans</b> (his example for size) ≈ <b>$5,000 today</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Two shorter Corinthian letters → pay <b>one fee</b>, one copy of both</li></ul>"},{"t":701.52,"lv":2,"h":"The Apostolicon and P46","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">By <b>100 AD</b> all Paul's epistles collected and circulating as a <b>unit</b></li><li class=\"d1\">book called the <b>Apostolicon</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Copy: <b>P46</b> - P = papyrus; 46th one found (modern name, not ancient)</li><li class=\"d1\">from about <b>130 AD</b></li><li class=\"d1\">contents = <b>exactly what we have</b>, nothing extra</li><li class=\"d1\">includes <b>Hebrews</b> - many, incl. church fathers, doubted Paul wrote it</li><li class=\"d1\">Hebrews doesn't name Paul as author - reason it counts as Pauline = it circulated in this collection</li><li class=\"d0\">In P46 Hebrews is <b>second after Romans</b></li><li class=\"d1\">ordered <b>longest to shortest</b> - source of our order</li><li class=\"d1\">Hebrews later moved toward the end because of the <b>doubts</b></li></ul>"},{"t":816.8,"lv":3,"h":"Gospels and general epistles as units","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Apostolicon = basis of our <b>epistle book</b> - read in worship after the Gospels</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Irenaeus of Lyon</b>: by <b>150</b> four Gospels collected, read as a unit</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>General / Catholic epistles</b> collected by ~<b>175</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Clement of Alexandria</b> wrote a commentary on them as a unit</li><li class=\"d0\">All end up conglomerating into the <b>New Testament</b></li></ul>"},{"t":879.66,"lv":2,"h":"Disputed authorship: Hebrews","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Disagreements about which epistles Paul wrote - some <b>ancient</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Hebrews</b> - fathers suggested many authors</li><li class=\"d1\">style <b>very different</b> from rest of Paul, yet ideas closely linked</li><li class=\"d1\">suggestions were Paul's associates: <b>St. Barnabas</b>, <b>St. Apollos</b></li><li class=\"d1\">oddest: obscure father <b>Fulgentius</b> - the <b>Theotokos</b> wrote it - \"safely set aside, but interesting\"</li><li class=\"d0\">In liturgy we still say <b>\"the epistle from St. Paul to the Hebrews\"</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Will argue that's <b>legitimate</b> - \"not fibbing or fudging\"</li></ul>"},{"t":961.9,"lv":2,"h":"A continuum of Pauline authorship","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Given how epistles were written and collected: \"Pauline authorship\" is a <b>range</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Never means he wrote it <b>by hand</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Galatians, Romans</b>: everyone, \"most crazed fundamentalist to most crazed liberal,\" agrees Paul wrote</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>direct reflection of Paul's mind</b>; scribe involved but straight from Paul</li><li class=\"d0\">Other end: <b>Hebrews</b> - doubted all through church history</li><li class=\"d0\">In between: <b>pastoral epistles</b> (1-2 Timothy, Titus) nearer Hebrews end</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Philemon</b> - everyone agrees Paul wrote it</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Ephesians, Colossians</b> in the middle - people go both ways</li></ul>"},{"t":1091.65,"lv":3,"h":"Ephesians and Colossians: a co-author","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">All epistles have a <b>formal introduction</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Romans 1:1</b>: author (<b>Paul</b>, bondservant of Jesus Christ), credentials, then recipients</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Ephesians, Colossians</b>: \"<b>Paul and Timothy</b> to the church\"</li><li class=\"d1\">Paul listed a <b>co-author</b></li><li class=\"d1\">explains Greek style not matching Galatians and Romans</li></ul>"},{"t":1157.11,"lv":3,"h":"Pastoral epistles and Luke","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>1-2 Timothy, Titus</b>: Greek different, <b>no co-author</b> listed</li><li class=\"d0\">Compare phrasing: much of Paul's usual phrasing is <b>the same</b> (easy with a computer)</li><li class=\"d0\">Differences <b>match St. Luke almost perfectly</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Luke mentioned as <b>with Paul</b> when written</li><li class=\"d1\">Paul writing from <b>prison in Rome</b> near end of life</li><li class=\"d0\">→ likely <b>Luke as amanuensis</b>, or helping produce them</li><li class=\"d0\">Still <b>coming from Paul</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1234.31,"lv":3,"h":"Hebrews as a sermon","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Question whether Hebrews is an <b>epistle</b> at all - <b>no opening formula</b>, no \"Paul to...\"</li><li class=\"d1\">\"immediately begins just with the text\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Style fits current <b>homiletical / rhetorical</b> style - <b>more sermon than letter</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Compare Paul's <b>sermons in Acts</b> → lots of crossover in <b>phrasing and syntax</b></li><li class=\"d1\">maybe Paul's preaching style; maybe <b>Luke transcribing</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Does <b>conclude</b> like an epistle</li><li class=\"d0\">His argument (more in Hebrews): a <b>sermon of Paul's, transcribed</b> and circulated in written form</li><li class=\"d0\">Summary: <b>all</b> are Paul's - different <b>scribes, deliverers, intermediaries</b> explain differences in style and format</li></ul>"},{"t":1392.36,"lv":2,"h":"Romans: background and the Claudius expulsion","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Romans: nobody doubts it comes <b>directly from Paul</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Paul says he has <b>not been to Rome yet</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Much on <b>reconciling Jewish Christians and Gentile Christians</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Event behind it (most scholars): <b>Acts</b> - <b>Priscilla and Aquila</b> at Corinth because <b>expelled from Rome</b> by <b>Emperor Claudius</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>all Jews</b> expelled - he did it twice (\"so effective the first one\")</li><li class=\"d1\">around <b>54</b>; relented, let them back within about a year</li><li class=\"d0\">Rome church began with <b>Pentecost</b> pilgrims: accepted Jesus as Christ, returned to Rome</li><li class=\"d1\">→ a <b>Christian synagogue</b> that became the church; Gentiles came later</li><li class=\"d0\">While Jews were out, <b>Gentile portion ran independently ~a year</b></li><li class=\"d1\">returning Jewish Christians must <b>reconnect</b>; flavor of community changed</li></ul>"},{"t":1556.71,"lv":3,"h":"Paul's aim: unity of the church","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Paul doesn't want <b>two separate churches</b>, Jewish and Gentile</li><li class=\"d0\">Wants <b>unity preserved</b> - much of Romans aimed at it</li><li class=\"d0\">Both groups in the <b>same predicament</b>, find salvation in Christ the <b>same way</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Jews, pagan Gentiles, or <b>God-fearers</b> (associated with synagogue, not fully Jewish)</li></ul>"},{"t":1609.89,"lv":3,"h":"Writing to a church he hasn't visited","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Unlike most epistles: Paul <b>hasn't been there</b></li><li class=\"d1\">usually founded the church (e.g. <b>Corinth, Ephesus</b>) and writes further instruction, problems</li><li class=\"d0\">Romans: \"<b>writing blind</b>\" about situations he knows, to people he mostly doesn't</li><li class=\"d0\">Q: how well informed? would they expect a letter? - don't know about expectation</li><li class=\"d1\">close associates <b>Priscilla and Aquila</b> were part of the Roman church, expelled</li><li class=\"d1\">Paul's <b>home base</b> in Antioch / Jerusalem between journeys - went back for <b>Passover, Pentecost</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Jews incl. Christian Jews from everywhere there; met him and other apostles</li><li class=\"d0\">Epistles to founded churches: <b>specific situations</b>, name people (bad or good leaders)</li><li class=\"d1\">Romans: <b>almost none</b> of that - lacks familiarity</li></ul>"},{"t":1764.85,"lv":2,"h":"Epistles are occasional documents","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Gospels and Acts = <b>narrative</b>; epistles <b>not a story</b>, a letter</li><li class=\"d0\">Also aimed at <b>particular situations</b> = <b>occasional documents</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Luke had no particular incident prompting Gospel / Acts - people in general needed gospel and early church history</li><li class=\"d0\">Paul's epistles: <b>series of particular situations</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Romans less so - the <b>Jew and Gentile</b> situation</li><li class=\"d1\">not like <b>1 Corinthians</b>: excommunicate a person for sins</li></ul>"},{"t":1862.14,"lv":3,"h":"Not a systematic theology","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Many <b>Protestants</b> don't read Romans as occasional - call it Paul's <b>systematic theology</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Ancient <b>treatises</b> have their own form: <i>On the Soul</i>, <i>On the Resurrection</i> - fathers wrote them</li><li class=\"d1\">Paul's, if any, aren't in the NT</li><li class=\"d0\">Romans = <b>letter to a church</b>, not abstract ideas - things pertaining to their situation</li><li class=\"d0\">Analogy: bishop writing a parish <b>between priests</b> how to handle situations</li></ul>"},{"t":1957.83,"lv":3,"h":"Not just an artifact either","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Not merely a relic of 1st-c. Rome problems</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Human nature is one</b> - their problems not so different from ours</li><li class=\"d0\">So also <b>Paul addressing us</b> - in a <b>pastoral way</b>, not \"rationalistic, let's muse about theology\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Terms like <b>election</b>: abstract, or particular situations?</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Check</b>: if our reading <b>would have made no sense to a 1st-c. Christian in Rome</b>, we're wrong - \"rabbit trail\"</li></ul>"},{"t":2049.19,"lv":2,"h":"Reception history: Luther and translations","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">His treatment will be <b>very different</b> from others you may know</li><li class=\"d0\">Mostly treated by Protestants: reading <b>Romans in Greek</b> set off <b>Martin Luther</b> → <b>Protestant Reformation</b> - \"not a coincidence\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Luther applied part of Romans to his situation with <b>Rome</b> in medieval Europe</li><li class=\"d1\">legitimate or not - later</li><li class=\"d0\">Shaped reading ~<b>500 years</b>: Protestants, and Catholics in response</li><li class=\"d0\">Even <b>Chrysostom</b> on Romans - translations by Protestants like <b>Philip Schaff</b></li><li class=\"d1\">not nefarious - \"how they've imbibed it\"</li></ul>"},{"t":2167.39,"lv":2,"h":"Reading Paul through his use of the Old Testament","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Biggest influence (with the fathers): <b>how Paul uses the OT</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Paul <b>quotes constantly</b>, alludes where not quoting</li><li class=\"d0\">Also <b>Jewish traditions of his time</b> in which he's trained</li><li class=\"d0\">Example: <b>1 Corinthians</b> - the <b>rock followed them</b>, \"that rock was Christ\"</li><li class=\"d1\">we skip \"<b>followed</b>\"; it's a <b>rabbinic Midrash</b> tradition</li><li class=\"d1\">question: water for <b>40 years</b>? Moses struck rock at start, and again at end (judged)</li><li class=\"d1\">solution: the rock followed - Paul mentions it <b>offhandedly</b></li></ul>"},{"t":2283.95,"lv":3,"h":"No sola scriptura for Paul","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Paul had <b>no sola scriptura</b> idea</li><li class=\"d0\">Read text <b>within the body of tradition</b> he received - <b>no firm line</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>1 Timothy</b>: <b>Jannes and Jambres</b> contested Moses</li><li class=\"d1\">not in the OT - from religious literature: <b>Pharaoh's court magicians</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Paul not endorsing all those stories as scripture, nor dismissing because not in Torah</li><li class=\"d0\">Paul's way = basically the <b>Orthodox way</b>: no scripture / tradition polarity</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Roman Catholic</b>: two separate things, church authority picks</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Protestant</b>: scripture only, tradition suspicious</li><li class=\"d1\">Orthodox: books <b>handed down</b>; <b>tradition says</b> these are scripture - those read in church</li></ul>"},{"t":2428.44,"lv":2,"h":"Bias: Paul interprets Scripture correctly","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">His bias: <b>Paul interprets Scripture correctly</b> - sounds bizarre to have to say</li><li class=\"d0\">Whole books on how NT interprets OT - treated as <b>oddity</b></li><li class=\"d1\">moderns: \"we can tell what the OT means\"; apostles \"doing something weird\"</li><li class=\"d1\">conservatives: apostles had <b>authority</b> to do it - \"authority to do it wrong\"</li><li class=\"d0\">His method: <b>go to the OT</b>, see what the passage means there</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>that</b> shows what Paul is saying - don't guess Paul's use and read back</li></ul>"},{"t":2523.33,"lv":3,"h":"Example: Jacob I loved, Esau I hated","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>Romans 9</b> opening quotes the prophet: <b>\"Jacob I have loved, Esau I have hated\"</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Calvinists</b> build <b>predestination</b> on it - before born, God loved one, hated other</li><li class=\"d0\">Original text: nothing to do with <b>individuals</b></li><li class=\"d1\">about <b>nations of Israel and Edom</b>; God chose <b>Israel</b> as his people, not Edomites</li><li class=\"d0\">Will return - incl. what <b>\"hated\"</b> means</li><li class=\"d0\">Use OT to understand Paul's point</li></ul>"},{"t":2603.91,"lv":3,"h":"Q&amp;A: Hearing the Greek Old Testament","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Q: Roman recipients, at least Jewish, would have <b>same OT interpretation</b> as Paul?</li><li class=\"d0\">Yes - also <b>God-fearers</b> in the synagogue</li><li class=\"d0\">In Rome synagogues used <b>Hebrew or Greek</b>, not Aramaic → <b>Greek OT</b> tradition heard and preached</li><li class=\"d0\">Paul cites a <b>religious tradition of worship</b> he and audience share</li><li class=\"d1\">they knew <b>Jannes and Jambres</b> immediately</li><li class=\"d0\">They had <b>heard these texts preached</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Still Christian synagogues: <b>Saturday</b> synagogue, readings and preaching; <b>Sunday</b> Christians gather for <b>Eucharist</b></li><li class=\"d1\">later 2nd c., expelled from synagogue → move both onto Sunday = <b>divine liturgy</b> with two parts</li><li class=\"d0\">Preaching was <b>Christian interpretation</b></li></ul>"},{"t":2772.84,"lv":2,"h":"Paul's \"conversion\"","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Reminder from <b>Acts</b>: \"conversion\" (scare quotes) - <b>Augustine, Luther</b> saw leaving one religion for another</li><li class=\"d0\">Road to Damascus ~<b>35 AD</b>, two years after resurrection</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>not yet two religions</b>, Judaism and Christianity</li><li class=\"d0\">Conversion as <b>repentance</b> - yes; as changing religion - no</li><li class=\"d0\">Saw Christ as the <b>Messiah he'd been expecting</b> - as a <b>Pharisee</b></li><li class=\"d1\">what changes: <b>\"he has come\"</b>, Jesus of Nazareth</li></ul>"},{"t":2856.74,"lv":3,"h":"Dominoes and approach to fellow Jews","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Everything else = <b>domino</b>: Messiah has come, died, risen → <b>last days</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Gentiles turn, <b>Israel returns</b>, Gentiles stream into Jerusalem</li><li class=\"d1\">all believed the day <b>before</b> Damascus - now \"they're happening\" = <b>shift in his timetable</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Calls himself a <b>Pharisee in present tense</b> - once in Romans, once elsewhere</li><li class=\"d0\">Approach to Jews: not \"abandon false religion\" but <b>\"the Messiah you've been waiting for has come\"</b></li><li class=\"d1\">hopes they make the same acknowledgement</li></ul>"}]});
